{
  "title": "Flattest Mountain Pass Queries",
  "description": "## Task\n\nHikers plan trips across a mountain range with `n` huts joined by two-way trails. Each trail has a steepness. A trip from one hut to another follows some sequence of trails, and its difficulty is the steepest trail on it. For every trip, find the smallest difficulty any route could have. A trip from a hut to itself has difficulty `0`. If no route exists the answer is `-1`.\n\n## Input\n\n- `n` — the number of huts, numbered `0` to `n - 1`.\n- `trails` — a list of `[a, b, steepness]`, each a two-way trail. Parallel trails may appear.\n- `trips` — a list of `[s, t]` hut pairs.\n\n## Output\n\nA list with one integer per trip, in order.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nflattest_pass_queries(4, [[0, 1, 5], [1, 2, 3], [0, 2, 9]], [[0, 2], [0, 0], [0, 3]])  # returns [5, 0, -1]\n```\n\n## Constraints\n\n- 1 ≤ `n` ≤ 1.5 * 10^4\n- 0 ≤ number of trails ≤ 4 * 10^4\n- 0 ≤ number of trips ≤ 10^4\n- 1 ≤ each steepness ≤ 10^6\n- Searching the graph again for every trip is too slow on the largest tests.\n",
  "signature": "def flattest_pass_queries(n: int, trails: list[list[int]], trips: list[list[int]]) -> list[int]",
